  "xeRAbora, live demo of the client's page": "",
  "Questions people ask first.": "",
  "Does it change the game?": "",
  "No. The in-game core reads memory and writes nothing into the game or the image. During development a debug build poked a value into a HUD counter; that code went away before alpha.3, and the release core is read-only.": "",
  "Is this cheating? Will RA accept the unlocks?": "",
  "The unlocks go through stock rcheevos in softcore mode, the same runtime the supported emulators use. We announced the project on the RetroAchievements forum and discussed it with the rcheevos maintainers. The client does not claim hardcore; that would need the RA team's approval.": "",
  "Which games work?": "",
  "Any PS2 game with an achievement set. The check in the game's menu tells you the counts for your game before you play: total, unlocked, and how many cannot unlock on real hardware yet.": "",
  "The unlock showed on the page but the TV did not flash.": "",
  "Look for <code>console acknowledged unlock notice</code> in <code>xerabora.log</code>. If it is missing, the notice never reached the console: same subnet, and the client must be running while you play (it can be restarted mid-game). If it is there, the console got it; tell us the game.": "",
  "Why does the PC say \"skipped by console\"?": "",
  "A snapshot the console produced and the PC never got. Since alpha.8 the count stays at zero or close to it during play; before it the console lost one snapshot in twelve to its own send loop. A number that keeps growing means the console is short of time: a game hammering the USB stick, or a busy transmitter. rcheevos evaluates the snapshots that arrive.": "",
  "Can another console use this?": "",
  "Yes. The PC client knows nothing about the PS2 beyond the memory it receives. Write an agent that reads your console's memory, computes the RA hash and speaks <a href=\"https://github.com/hacan359/xerabora/blob/main/protocol/PROTOCOL.md\">the protocol</a>, and xerabora does the rest: the library, the live view, the unlocks.": "",
  "Can I follow the game from my phone?": "",
  "Two ways. The Android app runs the client on the phone itself: the console finds the phone on the Wi-Fi as it finds a PC, the phone unlocks the achievements, and each unlock shows up as a notification. With the client on a PC, press OPEN TO THE NETWORK on SETTINGS and open the page on the phone's browser; it shows LIVE and everything else, while the login and QUIT stay on the PC.": "",
  "Do I need the console at all?": "",
  "No. Without one, xeRAbora is a RetroAchievements client: your library, each game's achievements, leaderboards and profile from your Web API key. The console adds the live half.": "",
  "Built on Open PS2 Loader, rcheevos and ps2sdk, tested on a real console. The OPL fork keeps OPL's license (AFL 3.0); the PC client is MIT; rcheevos is MIT.": "",
  "The OPL logo belongs to the Open PS2 Loader project and the RA mark to RetroAchievements; they appear here to show what this project connects.": "",
